[SIZE="4"][B]RPQuest[/B][/SIZE]
[SIZE="4"][COLOR="DarkRed"][B]Notice:[/B][/COLOR] I'm actively developing this. Being a social-focused addon there are many aspects that I need to test with others. If you are willing to help test it or have comments, contact me![/SIZE]

A native, [B]zero-dependency[/B] add-on for [B]The Elder Scrolls Online[/B] that lets you create, play, complete, and share [B]custom Roleplay quests[/B] directly inside ESO's native UI.

[SIZE="3"][B]Key Features[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][B]Native Quest Journal Integration (J):[/B] Adds a dedicated [B]RPQuests[/B] tab inside the standard Quest Journal with exclusive accordion sections for [B]Pending[/B], [B]Quests[/B], [B]Completed[/B], [B]Preparing[/B], and [B]Sent[/B] (keeping an active section expanded whenever quests exist and remembering the last open section and selected quest across sessions), plus native Journal keybind shortcuts for [B]Show on Map[/B] and [B]Abandon[/B].
[*][B]Reticle NPC Target Capture:[/B] Aim at any living NPC and press your keybind to register objectives automatically. Capturing the same NPC name multiple times stacks into [I]Imp x3[/I]; different named NPCs are managed individually from the editor's target dropdown (with delete selected and delete all buttons), per-target [B]Map Search Area Size[/B] ([I]Tiny[/I] / [I]Small[/I] / [I]Medium[/I] / [I]Large[/I] / [I]Huge[/I]), an [B]Optional target[/B] checkbox, per-target [B]Location[/B] conditions ([B]Required[/B] within [I]Tiny[/I] / [I]Small[/I] / [I]Medium[/I] / [I]Large[/I] / [I]Huge[/I] ([I]8m[/I] / [I]23m[/I] / [I]39m[/I] / [I]54m[/I] / [I]70m[/I]) or optional—eliminating a [B]Required[/B] target outside the radius marks the quest as [I]Failed[/I]), and a 3-state [B]Elimination Message[/B] selector ([I]On elimination[/I], [I]On elimination (location met)[/I], [I]On elimination (location missed)[/I]).
[*][B]Hidden Discovery Points:[/B] Stand at an exploration spot and click [B]+ Point Here[/B] in the editor's [B]Hidden Discovery Points (Optional)[/B] accordion with a configurable trigger radius dropdown ([I]Tiny[/I] / [I]Small[/I] / [I]Medium[/I] / [I]Large[/I] / [I]Huge[/I] — [I]8m[/I], [I]23m[/I], [I]39m[/I], [I]54m[/I], [I]70m[/I]) and a required [B]Discovery Point Message[/B]. Creators automatically see purple discovery areas on the map (with radius and message summary tooltips), while players keep them hidden by default with an optional spoiler-free toggle ([B]Show discovery areas on map[/B]).
[*][B]Stealth / Undetected Condition (Disabled / Optional / Required) & Completion Message:[/B] Choose via dropdown whether staying undetected (no Justice bounty, infamy, or failed pickpocket in the quest area) is [B]Disabled[/B], [B]Optional[/B] (records detection without failing), or [B]Required[/B] (immediately fails the quest if detected), and optionally set a custom [B]Completion Message[/B] displayed when the quest finishes.
[*][B]World Map Search, Location & Discovery Areas (M):[/B] Supports per-target emerald-green search circles on the World Map with a brief initial pulse, deterministic fixed random offset, and automatic radius scaling on city/subzone maps, optional soft-orange captured target location circles (both layers clear per target as each objective is completed), and purple discovery point circles (automatically shown for creators in Edit/Preparing/Sent and hidden by default for players, clearing each point as it is discovered). In the editor's [B]Mission Settings[/B] accordion, [B]Recommend hiding areas on map[/B] (enabled by default on new drafts) sets a Master recommendation notice for immersion; for players, [B]Show search areas on map[/B], [B]Show location areas on map[/B], and [B]Show discovery areas on map[/B] are all off by default and can be toggled independently per quest in the quest details (while [B]Show on Map[/B] remains available whenever a map/area is defined). When zoomed out to a parent zone or Tamriel world map, displays a medium pulsing emerald reference area with a deterministic offset (keeping the objective inside without revealing its exact spot), and automatically hides it when zooming back into the target map or upon completion/deactivation.
[*][B]Native NPC Dialogue Injection:[/B] Open dialogue ([B]E[/B]) with up to 4 native ESO NPCs and click [B]+ Last NPC[/B] in the editor to bind custom RPQuest dialogue lines and per-NPC player response options, injected seamlessly into the native interaction window without altering original NPC options.
[*][B]Combat & Discovery Tracking + Native Alerts:[/B] Tracks kills and exploration proximity, triggering native Center Screen Announcements and official quest sounds.
[*][B]Local Quest Master Testing & Reset:[/B] Use [B]Test Quest[/B] / [B]Stop Testing[/B] on any [B]Preparing[/B] or [B]Sent[/B] quest to test the full flow locally before sending, [B]Reset[/B] to restart a prepared or [I]Failed[/I] quest, or [B]Migrate[/B] if an older draft needs schema updating.
[*][B]1-to-1 In-Game Mail Flow (No Copy/Paste):[/B] Assign a single [I]@AccountName[/I] recipient (and optional [I]To (Character)[/I] name) in the quest editor and click [B]Send Quest[/B] from the quest detail pane—sending directly when [I]@AccountName[/I] is already set, or opening the Mail Send pane with an optional roleplay message and live 1–6 part counter when no recipient is set (ESO prevents sending mail to your own account). Incoming quest mails are automatically ingested into the player's [B]Pending[/B] list with an alert and [B]Accept[/B] / [B]Reject[/B] buttons (mismatched-language quest mails are automatically rejected with an error alert). Accepting, completing, failing, or rejecting a quest automatically mails the result back to the Quest Master, updating the contract's resolution ([I]Accepted[/I] / [I]Completed[/I] / [I]Failed[/I] / [I]Rejected[/I]) separately from its lifecycle ([I]Draft[/I] / [I]Sent[/I] / [I]Received[/I]).
[*][B]Account-Wide Persistence & Auto-Localization:[/B] Stores quests, progress, and last open Journal section / selected quest account-wide via SavedVariables (matching ESO's account-wide mail, with automatic migration from legacy per-character data) and auto-detects all 7 official ESO client languages (English, German, Spanish, French, Japanese, Russian, Simplified Chinese).
[/LIST]

[SIZE="3"][B]Keybinds (Settings -> Controls -> RPQuest - Custom Quests)[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][B]Capture Target Under Reticle (NPC):[/B] Captures the living NPC currently under your reticle into the quest draft.
[*][B]Open / Close Custom Quests Tab:[/B] Opens or closes the Quest Journal directly on the [B]RPQuests[/B] tab.
[/LIST]

[SIZE="3"][B]Quick Start[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST=1]
[*][B]Create a Quest:[/B] Open the Quest Journal ([B]J[/B]), switch to the [B]RPQuests[/B] tab, and click [B]+ New[/B] (which immediately creates a [B]New Quest[/B] draft in [B]Preparing[/B] and opens it in the editor). Use the single-open animated accordion sections to set Title, [B]Description / Story[/B], per-target [B]Map Search Area Size[/B], [B]Optional target[/B], [B]Location[/B], and 3-state [B]Elimination Message[/B], plus [B]Completion Message[/B], [B]Undetected Stealth Condition[/B] ([I]Disabled[/I], [I]Optional[/I], or [I]Required[/I]), and [B]Recommend hiding areas on map[/B] in [B]Mission Settings[/B], as well as optional [B]NPC Dialogues[/B]. Aim at NPCs and press your capture keybind, or stand at discovery spots and click [B]+ Point Here[/B] in [B]Hidden Discovery Points (Optional)[/B] (each discovery point requires a [B]Discovery Point Message[/B]). In-progress edits are automatically preserved when switching quests or closing the journal, or click [B]Save Draft[/B] to save and finish editing (at least one discovery point or NPC target is required before testing or sending; use [B]Abandon[/B] in the bottom keybind strip to delete a draft).
[*][B]Send to a Player:[/B] Set the recipient [I]@AccountName[/I] (and optional character name) in the quest editor, select the saved quest in [B]Preparing[/B], and click [B]Send Quest[/B] in the quest detail pane. The player receives an alert and can [B]Accept[/B] or [B]Reject[/B] the quest directly from [B]Pending[/B] in their [B]RPQuests[/B] tab. Note: quests require the same game client language as the creator so NPC objectives and dialogues match.
[*][B]Play & Auto-Report:[/B] Accepting the quest activates tracking and injected NPC dialogues immediately; map markers are off by default and can be toggled independently per quest in the quest details ([B]Show search areas on map[/B] / [B]Show location areas on map[/B] / [B]Show discovery areas on map[/B]). Accepting, completing, or rejecting the quest automatically mails the result back to the Quest Master.
[/LIST]

[SIZE="3"][B]How to Use / Guide[/B][/SIZE]

[B]1. Roles: Quest Master vs. Player[/B]
[LIST]
[*][B]Quest Master (Creator):[/B] Authors custom quests in [B]Preparing[/B], captures NPC targets or discovery spots in the world, tests locally with [B]Test Quest[/B], configures optional rules or dialogues, and dispatches contracts to a player via in-game mail. Once sent, the Quest Master tracks live progress and final outcomes in [B]Sent[/B].
[*][B]Player (Recipient):[/B] Receives quest offers in [B]Pending[/B], reviews the briefing, and chooses [B]Accept[/B] or [B]Reject[/B]. Active quests are tracked automatically in-world, and results are mailed back to the Quest Master with zero manual copy/paste.
[/LIST]

[B]2. Journal Sections (Exclusive Accordion)[/B]
The [B]RPQuests[/B] tab inside your Quest Journal ([B]J[/B]) uses an exclusive accordion—expanding one section collapses the others so your list stays tidy (one active section stays expanded whenever quests exist, and your last open section and selected quest are restored across sessions):
[LIST]
[*][B]Pending:[/B] Incoming quest offers waiting for you to [B]Accept[/B] or [B]Reject[/B].
[*][B]Quests:[/B] Your currently accepted and active quests (including [I]Failed[/I] quests that can be restarted with [B]Reset[/B]). Select a quest to view objectives, see any Master map-hiding recommendation notice, toggle [B]Show search areas on map[/B] / [B]Show location areas on map[/B] / [B]Show discovery areas on map[/B], toggle tracking, jump to the map ([B]Show on Map[/B]), [B]Abandon[/B], or manually complete it once all mandatory objectives are done.
[*][B]Completed:[/B] Archive of finished quests you have played, including final stealth and optional objective outcomes.
[*][B]Preparing:[/B] Unsent quest drafts you are creating or editing as a Quest Master. You can also copy any [B]Preparing[/B] or [B]Sent[/B] quest into [B]Preparing[/B] to create a fresh editable draft, test it locally ([B]Test Quest[/B]), or migrate legacy schemas ([B]Migrate[/B]).
[*][B]Sent:[/B] Contracts you have already mailed to players, showing [I]From:[/I] / [I]To:[/I] names, lifecycle and live resolution ([I]Sent[/I], [I]Accepted[/I], [I]Rejected[/I], [I]Completed[/I], [I]Failed[/I]), and automatic completion/failure results (such as [I]Undetected[/I] vs [I]Detected[/I] and optional objectives).
[/LIST]

[B]3. Quest Building Blocks[/B]
[LIST]
[*][B]Targets (NPC Elimination):[/B] Aim at any living NPC and press your [B]Capture Target Under Reticle (NPC)[/B] keybind. Capturing the same NPC name multiple times stacks into a count (e.g. [I]Imp x3[/I]); different named NPCs become separate objectives managed from the target dropdown (with delete selected and delete all buttons). For each target, you can set [B]Map Search Area Size[/B] ([I]Tiny[/I] / [I]Small[/I] / [I]Medium[/I] / [I]Large[/I] / [I]Huge[/I]), toggle [B]Optional target[/B] (allowing the player to finish the quest once all mandatory objectives are complete), configure [B]Location[/B] ([B]Capture Loc[/B], [I]Tiny[/I] / [I]Small[/I] / [I]Medium[/I] / [I]Large[/I] / [I]Huge[/I] ([I]8m[/I] / [I]23m[/I] / [I]39m[/I] / [I]54m[/I] / [I]70m[/I]) radius, and the [B]Required[/B] checkbox—unchecked means optional/anywhere, checked fails the quest if eliminated outside the radius), and select a 3-state [B]Elimination Message[/B] ([I]On elimination[/I], [I]On elimination (location met)[/I], [I]On elimination (location missed)[/I]).
[*][B]Hidden Discovery Points:[/B] Stand at an exploration spot and click [B]+ Point Here[/B] in the [B]Hidden Discovery Points (Optional)[/B] accordion. You can configure the trigger radius ([I]Tiny[/I] / [I]Small[/I] / [I]Medium[/I] / [I]Large[/I] / [I]Huge[/I] — [I]8m[/I], [I]23m[/I], [I]39m[/I], [I]54m[/I], or [I]70m[/I]) and must write a [B]Discovery Point Message[/B] before saving, testing, or sending. Discovery points remain hidden on the map and compass to avoid spoilers.
[*][B]Stealth (Undetected) & Completion Message:[/B] Use the [B]Undetected Stealth Condition[/B] dropdown in the quest editor ([I]Disabled[/I], [I]Optional[/I], or [I]Required[/I]) to track stealth as a bonus condition or fail the quest immediately upon detection. You can also enter an optional [B]Completion Message[/B] shown upon finishing.
[*][B]World Map Search & Location Areas (M):[/B] Displays per-target approximate search circles using a fixed random offset (with automatic radius scaling on city/subzone maps) and optional soft-orange captured target location circles; circles clear per target as each objective is completed (as well as when the quest is completed or deactivated). Both map layers are off by default for players and can be toggled independently per quest in the quest details (where any Master recommendation from [B]Recommend hiding areas on map[/B] is also shown); [B]Show on Map[/B] ([B]M[/B]) remains available whenever a map/area is defined.
[*][B]NPC Dialogues:[/B] Open dialogue ([B]E[/B]) with up to 4 native ESO NPCs and click [B]+ Last NPC[/B] to capture them into the quest's [B]NPC Dialogues[/B] section, then write a custom player response option and NPC reply for each. When the player speaks to those NPCs, your custom line is injected seamlessly into the native dialogue window without modifying original game options.
[/LIST]

[B]4. In-Game Mail Flow (Automatic)[/B]
[LIST]
[*][B]Sending:[/B] Enter the recipient's [I]@AccountName[/I] (and optional [I]To (Character)[/I] name) in the quest editor, save the quest, and click [B]Send Quest[/B] in the quest detail pane. When [I]@AccountName[/I] is already set in the editor, clicking [B]Send Quest[/B] sends directly; if no recipient is set, the Mail Send pane opens with an [I]Optional Roleplay Message[/I] field and a live part counter (1–6 parts). Both players must be on the same megaserver (e.g. PC-NA or PC-EU) and use the same client language, and ESO blocks sending mail to your own account.
[*][B]Arrival Announcement:[/B] When a quest mail arrives, RPQuest ingests it automatically and displays an on-screen announcement alerting the player that a new quest is waiting in [B]Pending[/B] (or rejects and alerts if the quest language does not match the client).
[*][B]Accepting & Rejecting:[/B] Clicking [B]Accept[/B] activates the quest and NPC dialogues (map markers remain off by default until toggled in the quest details), and notifies the Quest Master. Clicking [B]Reject[/B] declines the offer and notifies the Quest Master.
[*][B]Completion & Failure Reporting:[/B] When the player finishes all objectives (or manually completes a quest with only optional objectives remaining) or fails a required condition, RPQuest automatically mails the report back to the Quest Master and updates the contract's resolution in [B]Sent[/B].
[/LIST]

[SIZE="3"][B]Requirements[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][B]External Libraries:[/B] None (Zero-Dependency)
[*][B]Languages:[/B] English (en), German (de), Spanish (es), French (fr), Japanese (jp), Russian (ru), Simplified Chinese (zh)
[/LIST]

